From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: V <61495413+kelo221@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 16:45:07 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] AudioSource: stop on every live engine, never on a destroyed
 one

AudioSource::mSoloud kept pointing at the last engine a sound was played
on. Destroying or stopping the sound after that engine was destroyed used
freed memory (it hung or crashed on the freed mutex), and a sound played on
two engines was stopped only on one, leaving the other voice reading freed
samples when the sound was destroyed.

- Engines are kept in a process-wide list. AudioSource::stop() stops the
  sound on every live engine, under the list's lock; ~Soloud leaves the
  list first, so it waits for a stop in progress.
- Sound IDs are process-wide, so sounds first played on different engines
  no longer share an ID (stopping one stopped the other).
- BusInstance remembers its own engine. BusInstance and QueueInstance
  clear their parent's instance pointer and handle when destroyed, so Bus
  and Queue calls after the instance or its engine is gone do nothing
  instead of using freed memory. Playing a bus or queue again, on any
  engine, stops its old instance.
- A bus that never found its handle no longer stops every main-bus voice
  when its instance is destroyed.
- An engine's audio mutex now lives as long as the engine, and
  postinit_internal holds it, because a sound can be stopped on an engine
  from another thread while it is being initialized.
- SINGLE_INSTANCE stops the sound only on the engine playing it.
